<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.5 &amp; 1.6: Quadratic  and Composite Functions</title>

    <page>
        <alert>
        MTH 124
    </alert>

         <paragraphs>
            <title>Quadratic Functions</title>

        </paragraphs>
        <p workspace="0in">
            A <term>quadratic function</term> is one that may be written in the
            form
            <md>
                q(x)=ax^2 + bx + c
            </md>
            where <m>a \ne 0</m>.
            That is, there is an <m>x^2</m> term but no higher powers of
            <m>x</m>.
        </p>

        <activity>
            <title>Activity 1.5.2</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Open a browser and go to Desmos, <term>desmos.com</term>.
                    In a cell in Desmos, enter <m>q(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> in
                    exactly that form; you will be prompted to add sliders for
                    <m>a, b,</m> and <m>c</m>.
                    Do so.
                    Then begin exploring with the sliders and respond to the
                    following questions.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe how changing the value of <m>a</m> affects the
                        graph of <m>q</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe how changing the value of <m>b</m> affects the
                        graph of <m>q</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe how changing the value of <m>c</m> affects the
                        graph of <m>q</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Which parameter seems to have the simplest effect?
                        Which parameter seems to have the most complicated
                        effect?
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is it possible to find a formula for a quadratic
                        function that passes through the points
                        <m>(0,8), (1,12), (2,12)</m>?
                        If yes, do so; if not, explain why not.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <assemblage>
            <title>Quadratic Formula - Solutions to <m>q(x)=0</m></title>

            <p>
                Let <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> be real numbers with
                <m>a\ne0</m>.
                The equation <m>ax^2+bx+c =0</m> can have <m>0, 1</m>, or
                <m>2</m> real solutions.
                These real solutions are given by the
                <term>quadratic formula</term>,
                <md>
                    x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}
                </md>
                provided <m>b^2 - 4ac \ge 0</m>.
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <activity>
            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the solutions to <m>3x^2-18x-21=0</m> using the
                        quadratic formula.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <assemblage>
            <title>The vertex of a parabola</title>

            <p>
                The quadratic function <m>y=q(x) = ax^2+bx+c</m> has its
                <term>vertex</term> at the <m>x</m>-coordinate
                <m>\ds x = -\frac{b}{2a}</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                When <m>a&gt;0</m>, the graph <term>opens upward</term>, and
                thus the vertex is the lowest point on the graph of <m>q</m>
                (see example on the left below).
                When <m>a&lt;0</m>, the graph <term>opens downward</term>, and
                so the vertex is the highest point (see example on the right
                below).
            </p>

            <p>
                The graph is <em>symmetric</em> around the line
                <m>\ds x = -\frac{b}{2a}</m> (the vertical line that passes
                through the vertex!).
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <sidebyside widths="25% 25%">
            <image>
                <shortdescription>
                    A parabola that opens upward.
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-5-1">
                    <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            p(x)=1/5*(x-3)^2+4
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="[-4,-3,10,8]">
                            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                            <graph function="p"/>
                            <label anchor="(-2.2,7.5)">
                                <m>
                                    p(x)
                                </m>
                            </label>
                            <point p="(0,p(0))"/>
                            <point p="(6,p(6))"/>
                            <point offset="(1,-15)" p="(3,p(3))">
                                <m>
                                    (-\frac{b}{2a},p(-\frac{b}{2a}))
                                </m>
                            </point>
                            <line endpoints="((3,0),(3,3))" infinite="yes" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
                            <label anchor="(3,-2)" alignment="east">
                                <m>
                                    x=-\frac{b}{2a}
                                </m>
                            </label>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>

            <image>
                <shortdescription>
                    A parabola that opens downward.
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-5-2">
                    <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                        <definition>
                            h(x)=-1/5*(x-3)^2+4
                        </definition>
                        <coordinates bbox="[-4,-3,10,8]">
                            <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                            <graph function="h"/>
                            <label anchor="(-2.2,7.5)">
                                <m>
                                    h(x)
                                </m>
                            </label>
                            <point p="(0,h(0))"/>
                            <point p="(6,h(6))"/>
                            <point offset="(1,1)" p="(3,h(3))">
                                <m>
                                    (-\frac{b}{2a},h(-\frac{b}{2a}))
                                </m>
                            </point>
                            <line endpoints="((3,0),(3,3))" infinite="yes" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
                            <label anchor="(3,-2)" alignment="east">
                                <m>
                                    x=-\frac{b}{2a}
                                </m>
                            </label>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Vertex form of a quadratic function</title>

            <p>
                A quadratic function with vertex <m>(h,k)</m> may be written in
                the form <m>y = a(x-h)^2+k</m>.
                The constant <m>a</m> may be determined from one other function
                value for an input <m>x\ne h</m>.
            </p>
        </assemblage>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find an equation for the parabola that has the vertex
                        <m>(1,4)</m> and goes through the point <m>(3,2)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

        <p workspace="0.5in">
            Just a note: objects that fall under the force of gravity, like
            baseballs or water balloons or anything, can be modeled by quadratic
            curves.
        </p>

        <paragraphs>
            <title>Composite Functions</title>

        </paragraphs>

        <definition>
            <title>Definition 1.6.2</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are functions such that
                    <m>{g:A\rightarrow B}</m> and <m>{f:B\rightarrow C}</m>, we
                    define the <term>composition of <m>f</m> and <m>g</m></term>
                    to be the new function <m>{h:A\rightarrow C}</m> given by
                    <md>
                        h(t) = f(g(t)).
                    </md>
                    We also sometimes use the notation <m>{h = f\circ g}</m>
                    (said " <m>f</m> compose <m>g</m> " or " <m>f</m> of
                    <m>g</m> "), where <m>{f\circ g}</m> is the single function
                    defined by
                    <md>
                        (f\circ g)(t) = f(g(t))
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            It's a function within a function!
            Work inside out.
            Take the result of one function, and plug it right back into another
            function.
        </p>

        <example>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>f</m> is a function with <m>f(3)=4</m> and <m>g</m>
                is a function with <m>g(4)=5</m>.
                Let's look at
                <md>
                    g(f(3)).
                </md>
                Since we work from the inside out, and we know <m>f(3)=4</m>, we
                can replace <m>f(3)</m> with <m>4</m>, and so we can simplify
                just that part first:
                <md>
                    g(f(3)) = g(4).
                </md>
                But now we are looking for <m>g(4)</m> which we know is 5.
                So thinking in stages we have
                <md>
                    g(f(3)) = g(4) = 5.
                </md>
                On the other hand, if we were to switch and have
                <md>
                    f(g(3)),
                </md>
                we would need to take <m>g(3)</m> first since it is on the
                inside.
                We don't know what that is...
                So at the moment, we cannot simplify <m>f(g(3))</m> until we
                have more information.
            </p>
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <title>Activity 1.6.2</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let functions <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> be given by the graphs
                    below (which are each piecewise linear - that is, parts that
                    look like straight lines are straight lines) and let
                    <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be given by the table below.
                </p>

                <sidebyside widths="30% 70%">
                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            Two piecewise linear functions p and q.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-5-3">
                            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                                <definition>
                                    p1(x)=-2*(x+3)+3
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    p2(x)=1/2*(x+1)-1
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    p3(x)=2*(x-1)
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    q1(x)=3*(x+2.5)-2.5
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    q2(x)=2
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    q3(x)=-1*(x-1)+2
                                </definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-3.5,-3.5,3.5,3.5]">
                                    <grid-axes decorations="yes" hlabels="(-3,1,3)" vlabels="(-3,1,3)"/>
                                    <graph function="p1" domain="(-5,-1)"/>
                                    <graph function="p2" domain="(-1,1)"/>
                                    <graph function="p3" domain="(1,5)"/>
                                    <label anchor="(-2.75,3.2)">
                                        <m>
                                            p
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                    <graph function="q1" domain="(-5,-1)" stroke="green" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <graph function="q2" domain="(-1,1)" stroke="green" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <graph function="q3" domain="(1,5)" stroke="green" dash="4 4"/>
                                    <label anchor="(-2.75,-2.5)">
                                        <m>
                                            q
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>

                    <table>
                        <title>
                            A table of two functions <em>entirely UNRELATED</em>
                            to those graphed to the left.
                        </title>

                        <tabular halign="center">
                            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                                <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>0</cell>
                                <cell>6</cell>
                                <cell>1</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>1</cell>
                                <cell>4</cell>
                                <cell>3</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>2</cell>
                                <cell>3</cell>
                                <cell>0</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>3</cell>
                                <cell>4</cell>
                                <cell>4</cell>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>4</cell>
                                <cell>6</cell>
                                <cell>2</cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>
                </sidebyside>

                <p>
                    Compute each of the following quantities or explain why they
                    are not defined.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>p(q(0))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>q(p(0))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(p\circ p)(-1)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(f\circ g)(2)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>(g\circ f)(3)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>g(f(0))</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For what value(s) of <m>x</m> is <m>f(g(x)) = 4</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For what value(s) of <m>x</m> is <m>q(p(x)) = 1</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <title>Activity 1.6.3. Context</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>F = D(N) = 40+0.25N</m> be Dolbear’s function that
                    converts an input of number of chirps per minute to degrees
                    Fahrenheit.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Let <m>C = G(F) = \frac{5}{9} (F-32)</m> be the function
                    that converts an input of degrees Fahrenheit to an output of
                    degrees Celsius.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine a formula for the new function
                        <m>H = G(D(N))</m> that depends only on the variable
                        <m>N</m>.
                        That is <m>H</m> should equal some function of <m>N</m>
                        and only <m>N</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the meaning of the function you found in (a)?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="0.4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How does a plot of the new function <m>H = G(D(N))</m>
                        compare to that of Dolbear’s function?
                        Sketch a plot of this new function <m>H</m> on the blank
                        axes to the right of the plot of Dolbear’s function, and
                        discuss the similarities and differences between them.
                        Be sure to label the vertical scale on your axes.
                    </p>

                    <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 35%">
                        <image>
                            <shortdescription>
                                A plot of Dolbear's function, a linear function
                                with chirps per minute on the x-axis and temperature in
                                degrees Fahrenheit on the y-axis.
                            </shortdescription>
                            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-5-4">
                                <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                                    <definition>
                                        D(N)=10/40*(N-40)+50
                                    </definition>
                                    <coordinates bbox="[-20,-10,200,100]">
                                        <grid-axes xlabel="N (chirps/min)" ylabel="T (degrees Fahrenheit)" decorations="yes" hlabels="(40,40,160)" vlabels="(20,20,80)"/>
                                        <graph function="D" domain="(40,180)"/>
                                        <point p="(40,D(40))"/>
                                        <point p="(80,D(80))"/>
                                        <point p="(120,D(120))" alignment="southeast">
                                            <m>
                                                (120,70)
                                            </m>
                                        </point>
                                        <point p="(160,D(160))"/>
                                        <point p="(180,D(180))"/>
                                    </coordinates>
                                </diagram>
                            </prefigure>
                        </image>

                        <image>
                            <shortdescription>
                                A grid with the number of chirps per minute on the x-axis and the y-axis left blank for you to plot the composite function.
                            </shortdescription>
                            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-5-5">
                                <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                                    <coordinates bbox="[-20,-10,200,100]">
                                        <grid-axes xlabel="N (chirps/min)" decorations="yes" hlabels="(40,40,160)" vlabels="(-50,10,-50)"/>
                                    </coordinates>
                                </diagram>
                            </prefigure>
                        </image>
                    </sidebyside>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the domain of the function <m>H = G(D(N))</m>?
                        What is its range?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>

    <page>
        <activity>
            <title>Activity 1.6.4</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = 2x^2-3x+1</m> and <m>g(x) = \frac{5}{x}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>f(1+h)</m> for an unknown variable <m>h</m>
                        and expand and simplify the result as much as possible
                        by combining like terms.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Use part (a) to determine the most simplified expression
                        you can for the average rate of change of <m>f</m> on
                        the interval <m>[1,1+h]</m>.
                        That is, determine <m>AV_{[1,1+h]}</m> for <m>f</m> and
                        simplify the result as much as possible.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>g(1+h)</m>.
                        Is there any valid algebra you can do to write more
                        simply?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine the most simplified expression you can for the
                        average rate of change of <m>g</m> on the interval
                        <m>[1,1+h]</m>.
                        That is, determine <m>AV_{[1,1+h]}</m> for <m>g</m> and
                        simplify the result.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>
</handout>